**(2,1,1) distribution (ABB):** The Type A row has 7 in column $j$, 0 in $k, l$. Both Type B rows have 0 in $j$. Column $j$: $7+0+0=7$ ✓. The remaining $2\times 2$ sub-grid (columns $k,l$; rows $B_1, B_2$) has the same structure as the BBB diagonal case: all four pairs share one type $T$, giving $3 \times 2 = 6$ configurations.

Count: $3$ (choice of Type A row) $\times\ 3$ (choice of column $j$) $\times\ 6 = \mathbf{54}$.

